[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The Practice and the Dojo. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 8: Tim Ottinger from SCNA 2011 (2011).",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nTim was a cornerstone mentor at Object Mentor in Gurnee/Libertyville, teaching practitioners the daily habits of refactoring and intention-revealing naming.",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nWhat inspired Tim Ottinger to co-author 'Clean Code' with Uncle Bob? What did Object Mentor learn from embedding directly on client teams across the Midwest? What principles guided Tim Ottinger when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nRoll archive footage (YouTube ID: GYpgcyIH_gc). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nNaming things well is the primary interface between human programmers and LLMs. Meaningful names eliminate context-window ambiguity. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 9. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]